// Reading the Windows microphone permission, as the wizard sees it.
//
// This file is included into the [Code] section of Wheelhouse-Setup.iss, so
// everything in it is Pascal Script and takes Pascal comments -- a ';' comment
// here is read as code and fails the compile with "'BEGIN' expected".
//
// Two separate programs ask Windows the same question. The wizard asks it to
// decide whether to show the microphone-permission page at all; the engine
// (install-wheelhouse.ps1, Resolve-MicrophoneConsent) asks it to decide whether
// to print the closing advice about turning the microphone on. A machine where
// the two disagree shows the user a page about a problem the engine says they
// do not have, or stays silent about one they do.
//
// The part that can disagree is the decision, not the registry read, so the
// decision lives here on its own and takes the two values as arguments. The
// engine has a function of the same shape, and
// scripts/release/tests/test_installer.py runs both over one table of value
// pairs and requires the same answer from each. Reading the registry stays in
// MicrophoneAllowed, which is the part a test cannot drive without writing to
// the user's real permission setting.

function ResolveMicrophoneConsent(const First, Second: string): Boolean;
begin
  { First is the per-application value (the NonPackaged subkey), Second the
    umbrella one. Either is an empty string when Windows could not read it, when
    the key is absent, or when the value is present and empty -- Windows reports
    success on a value that exists and is empty, and an empty string is not
    'Allow', so a check that only asked whether the read succeeded would report
    a working microphone as blocked.

    An empty first value is not an answer, so the umbrella value is asked next.
    When neither answers, the result is "allowed": a Windows build that stores
    this somewhere else must not leave the user looking at a warning about a
    microphone that works. }
  Result := True;
  if First <> '' then
    Result := (CompareText(First, 'Allow') = 0)
  else if Second <> '' then
    Result := (CompareText(Second, 'Allow') = 0);
end;
